'''Line F''' of the [[CristoCarril]] (CC) system is a commuter rail line operated by [[Intercon]] that runs on the [[Old Grand Subdivision]] corridor between the [[Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal]] in [[Sant Cristòfor]] and [[L'Estany (CristoCarril)|L'Estany]] in [[Ciutat del Llac Sud]]. The line covers 85.4 km and serves 13 stations across two comarcas — [[Sant Cristòfor (comarca)|Sant Cristòfor]] and [[L'Estany (comarca)|L'Estany]]. It is the sixth CristoCarril line, complementing [[Line A (CristoCarril)|Line A]] on the [[Coastal Line]] corridor, [[Line B (CristoCarril)|Line B]] on the [[Portnou Line]] corridor, [[Line C (CristoCarril)|Line C]] on the [[Varakí Line]] corridor, [[Line D (CristoCarril)|Line D]] on the [[Via Sud Line]] corridor, and [[Line E (CristoCarril)|Line E]] on the [[Sant Bart Line]] corridor. Due to the steep gradients and tight curve radii of the switchback section on the Old Grand Subdivision, Line F is restricted to two-car train formations, the shortest of any CristoCarril line.

Line F follows a central-eastern route from the Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal, through the ciutats of Sant Cristòfor — [[Original (ciutat)|Original]] and [[Sant Jordi (ciutat)|Sant Jordi]] — before entering the [[L'Estany (comarca)|L'Estany comarca]] and serving the municipality of [[Ciutat del Llac Sud]]. The line shares the first ten stations with [[Line D (CristoCarril)|Line D]] on the Sant Bart Line and Old Grand Subdivision corridors before Line D diverges onto the Via Sud Subdivision at Polígon Sud, while Line F continues east along the Old Grand Subdivision through the switchback section into the interior highlands of Estret Province.

== Route ==

=== Sant Cristòfor ===

Line F begins at the [[Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal]] in [[Original (ciutat)|Original]], the city's primary rail hub, shared with [[Line B (CristoCarril)|Line B]], [[Line C (CristoCarril)|Line C]], [[Line D (CristoCarril)|Line D]], and [[Line E (CristoCarril)|Line E]]. From the terminal, the line heads south through three stations in Original — [[Portal de Sant Cristòfor Station|Portal de Sant Cristòfor]], [[Sant Pere (CristoCarril)|Sant Pere]], and [[La Font (CristoCarril)|La Font]] — serving the dense residential neighbourhoods south of the historic core. These four stations are shared with Lines B, C, D, and E. At La Font, Line B diverges to follow the [[Portnou Line]] corridor, while Line C continues on the [[Varakí Line]] corridor. Lines D, E, and F continue together on the [[Sant Bart Line]] corridor for one additional station to [[Mas Blanc (CristoCarril)|Mas Blanc]] in [[Sant Jordi (ciutat)|Sant Jordi]], where Line E continues north on the Sant Bart Line while Lines D and F diverge onto the Old Grand Subdivision, heading southeast through central Sant Jordi.

The line then serves five additional stations in Sant Jordi on the Old Grand Subdivision, shared with [[Line D (CristoCarril)|Line D]]: [[Estadi (CristoCarril)|Estadi]], [[Mercat Vell (CristoCarril)|Mercat Vell]], [[Carretera del Camp (CristoCarril)|Carretera del Camp]], [[Plaça dels Pins (CristoCarril)|Plaça dels Pins]], and [[Polígon Sud (CristoCarril)|Polígon Sud]]. Sant Jordi is a densely populated residential ciutat on the eastern side of Sant Cristòfor, and these stations provide the primary commuter rail connection for its central and southern neighbourhoods. At Polígon Sud, Line D diverges southward onto the Via Sud Subdivision toward [[Ciutat del Pujol (ciutat)|Ciutat del Pujol]] and [[Cumará (comarca)|Cumará]], while Line F continues east along the Old Grand Subdivision.

East of Polígon Sud, the line enters the less densely populated eastern districts of Sant Cristòfor comarca. The Old Grand Subdivision, originally constructed in the 1910s as a freight corridor serving the interior highlands, features numerous switchbacks as it climbs from the coastal plain into the interior highlands of Estret Province. The steep gradients and tight curve radii of the switchback section restrict Line F to two-car train formations, the shortest of any CristoCarril line, and limit maximum speeds to 80 km/h. The first F-exclusive station, [[La Colònia (CristoCarril)|La Colònia]], is located approximately 5 km east of Polígon Sud at the site of a former industrial colony. The second, [[Coll Alt (CristoCarril)|Coll Alt]], is situated at the mountain pass that marks the highest point on the line, approximately 27 km further east. Coll Alt is the highest elevated station in the CristoCarril system at approximately 185 metres above sea level, and its position at the crest of the pass offers views toward [[Sant Cristòfor]] and the coastal plain below. The switchback section between La Colònia and Coll Alt accounts for a significant portion of the line's total length relative to the direct distance, as the track winds through the steep terrain of the Serralada d'Estret foothills.

=== L'Estany ===

After leaving Coll Alt, the line enters the [[Sant Cristòfor–L'Estany Rail Tunnel]], a long tunnel bored through the Serralada d'Estret that carries the line beneath the highland divide and into the [[L'Estany (comarca)|L'Estany comarca]]. Upon emerging from the tunnel, Line F descends toward the interior lowlands surrounding the [[Estany de Panacú]]. The terminus, [[L'Estany (CristoCarril)|L'Estany]] station, is located in the municipality of [[Ciutat del Llac Sud]], a lakeside resort community on the southern shore of the Estany de Panacú. The station provides the primary commuter rail connection between the L'Estany comarca and Sant Cristòfor, supplementing the [[L'Estany Airport]] which serves the area's domestic aviation needs.

